Let me think about the Stan model first, then the installation.

The hierarchical model:
- y_i ~ Binomial(n_i, theta_i)
- theta_i ~ Beta(alpha, beta)
- Prior: (alpha, beta) ∝ (alpha + beta)^(-5/2)

This is a classic model. The prior (alpha+beta)^(-5/2) is from the "noninformative prior" for the Beta distribution (Klugkist et al. 2005). Actually the common one is (alpha+beta)^(-5/2). Let me just implement it.

In Stan:
```stan
data {
  int<lower=1> N;
  int<lower=0> y[N];
  int<lower=1> n[N];
}
parameters {
  vector<lower=0>[N] theta;
  real<lower=0> alpha;
  real<lower=0> beta;
}
model {
  for (i in 1:N) {
    y[i] ~ binomial(n[i], theta[i]);
    theta[i] ~ beta(alpha, beta);
  }
  target += -2.5 * log(alpha + beta);
}
```

Wait, the prior (alpha+beta)^(-5/2) means we add log of that: log((alpha+beta)^(-5/2)) = -2.5 * log(alpha+beta). Yes.

